- dispatch_allocated S (remove)
- candidate_vehicle_linked S vehicle_unit_gamma? Wait effect does not remove candidate_vehicle_linked. Actually execute_schedule_dispatch effect does not mention candidate_vehicle_linked. It only adds dispatched, vehicle_available, dispatch_slot_available. It does not remove candidate_vehicle_linked or dispatch_allocated? Let's check the definition:

(:action execute_schedule_dispatch
    :parameters (?candidate - schedule_candidate ?vehicle_unit - vehicle_unit ?dispatch_slot - dispatch_slot)
    :precondition
      (and
        (dispatch_allocated ?candidate)
        (candidate_vehicle_linked ?candidate ?vehicle_unit)
        (candidate_dispatch_slot_linked ?candidate ?dispatch_slot)
        (not
          (dispatched ?candidate)
      )
    :effect
      (and
        (dispatched ?candidate)
        (vehicle_available ?vehicle_unit)
        (dispatch_slot_available ?dispatch_slot)
      )
)

Thus effect does NOT remove dispatch_allocated or candidate_vehicle_linked or candidate_dispatch_slot_linked. So after execution, dispatch_allocated remains true, candidate_vehicle_linked remains true, candidate_dispatch_slot_linked remains true. However we also gain vehicle_available and dispatch_slot_available. The goal requires dispatch_slot_available dispatch_slot_alpha true (we will have). It does NOT require dispatch_allocated S to be false. So it's okay if dispatch_allocated remains true. However goal also requires vehicle_available vehicle_unit_gamma true (we will have). It does NOT require vehicle_allocated S to be false. So fine.

But note: we also have vehicle_allocated S true from init (vehicle_allocated shift_candidate_alpha). That's okay.

